            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2961"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.A.1.2",
            "text": "Utilize technology resources to gather information from primary and secondary sources.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2962"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.A.1.3",
            "text": "Define terms related to the social sciences.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2963"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.G.1",
        "domain_name": "Geography - The World in Spatial Terms",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.1.1",
            "text": "Use thematic maps, tables, charts, graphs, and photos to analyze geographic information.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2964"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.1.2",
            "text": "Review basic map elements (coordinate grid, cardinal and intermediate directions, title, compass rose, scale, key/legend with symbols).",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2965"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.1.3",
            "text": "Label the continents and oceans on a world map.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2966"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.1.4",
            "text": "Name and identify the purpose of maps (physical, political, elevation, population).",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2967"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.1.5",
            "text": "Compare maps and globes to develop an understanding of the concept of distortion.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2968"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.1.6",
            "text": "Use maps to identify different types of scale to measure distances between two places.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2969"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.G.2",
        "domain_name": "Geography - Places and Regions",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.2.1",
            "text": "Label the countries and commonwealths in North America (Canada, United States, Mexico) and in the Caribbean (Puerto Rico, Cuba, Bahamas, Dominican Republic, Haiti, Jamaica).",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2970"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.2.2",
            "text": "Identify the five regions of the United States.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2971"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.2.3",
            "text": "Label the states in each of the five regions of the United States.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2972"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.2.4",
            "text": "Describe the physical features of the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2973"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.2.5",
            "text": "Identify natural and man-made landmarks in the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2974"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.2.6",
            "text": "Investigate how people perceive places and regions differently by conducting interviews, mental mapping and studying news, poems, legends, and songs about a region or area.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2975"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.G.3",
        "domain_name": "Geography - Physical Systems",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.3.1",
            "text": "Describe the climate and vegetation in the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2976"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.3.2",
            "text": "Describe the natural resources in the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2977"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.G.4",
        "domain_name": "Geography - Human Systems",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.4.1",
            "text": "Explain how the environment influences settlement patterns in the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2978"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.4.2",
            "text": "Identify the cultures that have settled the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2979"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.4.3",
            "text": "Compare the cultural characteristics of diverse populations in one of the five regions of the United States with Canada, Mexico, or the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2980"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.G.4.4",
            "text": "Identify contributions from various ethnic groups to the United States.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2981"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.E.1",
        "domain_name": "Economics - Beginning Economics",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.E.1.1",
            "text": "Give examples of how scarcity results in trade.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2982"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.E.1.2",
            "text": "List the characteristics of money.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2983"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.E.1.3",
            "text": "Recognize that buyers and sellers interact to exchange goods and services through the use of trade or money.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2984"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.E.1.4",
            "text": "Distinguish between currencies used in the United States, Canada, Mexico, and the Caribbean.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-2985"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.CG.1",
        "domain_name": "Civics and Government - Foundations of Government",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.CG.1.1",
            "text": "Explain how the U.S. Constitution establishes the purpose and fulfills the need for government.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-16053"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.CG.1.2",
            "text": "Describe how the U.S. government gains its power from the people.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-16054"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "domain_id": "SS.3.CG.2",
        "domain_name": "Civics and Government - Civic and Political Participation",
        "standards": [
          {
            "id": "SS.3.CG.2.1",
            "text": "Describe how citizens demonstrate civility, cooperation, volunteerism, and other civic virtues.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-16055"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          },
          {
            "id": "SS.3.CG.2.2",
            "text": "Identify ways citizens can make a positive contribution to their community.",
            "text_mode": "exact",
            "citation_ids": ["c-cpalms-16056"],
            "page": null,
            "label": "A"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "assessment_notes": null,
    "key_learning_outcomes": {
      "label": "C",
      "items": [
        "Students analyze primary and secondary sources for historical inquiry.",
        "Students read and interpret maps, understanding continents, regions, and scale.",
        "Students describe physical features, climate, and natural resources of North America.",
        "Students understand basic economics: scarcity, trade, money, and buyer-seller interactions.",
        "Students learn about the U.S. Constitution and civic participation."
      ]
    },
